From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kv-cache <noreply@localhost>
Date: Mon, 17 Sep 2001 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] lmcache: gate LMCacheMPConnector's load path on num_external_tokens

The other half of patches/0010. Same one-line guard, applied to the copy that
ACTUALLY RUNS.

There are two near-identical LMCacheMPConnector implementations in this image
and the naming misleads. KVConnectorFactory registers "LMCacheMPConnector"
against vllm.distributed.kv_transfer.kv_connector.v1.lmcache_mp_connector, so
that looks like the live one -- but at the bottom of that module
_resolve_lmcache_mp_connector() does:

    if os.environ.get("LMCACHE_USE_UPSTREAM_MP"):  -> vLLM's builtin
    try: from lmcache.integration.vllm.lmcache_mp_connector import ...
                                                   -> THIS file  (default)
    except ImportError:                            -> vLLM's builtin

LMCache is installed, so the import succeeds and the DEFAULT path is this file.
The vLLM module logs "Using external LMCacheMPConnector from
lmcache.integration.vllm.lmcache_mp_connector" when it hands off. 0010 alone
therefore fixes only the LMCACHE_USE_UPSTREAM_MP / ImportError fallback and is
a no-op on a stock deployment -- it applies cleanly, the build succeeds, and the
bug survives. Both patches are needed; neither is redundant.

This was caught by running the mechanism check against an UNPATCHED image first
and watching it fail in the wrong file. It would not have been caught by
inspection, by a clean `git apply`, or by any needle count.

The bug is identical to 0010's: update_state_after_alloc() never reads
num_external_tokens, so a non-chosen MultiConnector sub-connector -- which since
vLLM #46865 receives the request's real blocks with num_external_tokens=0 --
decides purely on tracker.needs_retrieve() and wrongly moves
PREFETCHING -> WAITING_FOR_LOAD, creating a retrieve task for a load that must
not happen. The same `condition` then selects the lock-release range, so only
num_vllm_hit_tokens is freed instead of num_lmcache_hit_tokens and the
difference leaks; end_session() does not release lookup locks either.

This copy accounts in TOKENS (num_lmcache_hit_tokens / num_vllm_hit_tokens)
where the vLLM copy uses blocks * vllm_block_size, and is engine-group aware in
the block-tracking above the hunk. The guard is the same either way, and the
block tracking stays unconditional -- that is the store path.

Verify by mechanism, never by needles: the unguarded build answers correctly
while locks accumulate. See lab/verify_0010_guard.py, which drives the real
tracker and asserts non-chosen -> READY with all locks freed, chosen ->
WAITING_FOR_LOAD with only the vLLM prefix freed.

Upstream: vLLM #47505, LMCache #4028 (still open; #47294 was closed as the wrong
fix direction). Re-check both before dropping this.

Carried out-of-tree. See README.md.
---
--- a/lmcache/integration/vllm/lmcache_mp_connector.py
+++ b/lmcache/integration/vllm/lmcache_mp_connector.py
@@ -1138,7 +1138,11 @@
             tracker.append_block_ids(tuple(new_block_ids))
 
         # Update the state of the tracker
-        condition = tracker.needs_retrieve()
+        # Gate the LOAD path on num_external_tokens, not on `blocks`: since
+        # vLLM #46865 a non-chosen MultiConnector sub-connector receives the
+        # request's real blocks and must not load. 0 also frees ALL lookup
+        # locks below, which is what stops them leaking.
+        condition = num_external_tokens > 0 and tracker.needs_retrieve()
         if tracker.state == LMCacheMPRequestState.PREFETCHING:
             # If need to retrieve, change to WAITING_FOR_LOAD
             # Otherwise, change to READY
